# .gitignore — claude-corpus-engine
#
# This repository is the engine: filesystem conventions, GM rules, indexer and
# MCP servers. It deliberately carries no worldbuilding content. Everything
# excluded below is either private content, a derived artifact, machine-local,
# or a working document.

# --- Private content (carved out to its own repository at the 2026-09-04 split) ---
# World_Building/ is ignored wholesale. An earlier design shipped an empty
# scaffold plus World_Building/README.md via a negation; that is impossible here.
# World_Building/ is itself a git repository (the private content half), and git
# will not track a file inside a nested repository — `git add -f` on it silently
# stages nothing. Git cannot track empty directories either, so there is no
# scaffold to ship. The orientation document lives at the root instead, as
# World_Building_README.md, which this repository can actually carry.
/World_Building/
/Writing_Projects/
/Stories/
/Perchance_prompts/
/Other_References/
/Miscelanious_RPG_material/
/Sheet_Import/
/Trash/

# --- Derived artifacts (regenerable; never track) ---
/index/
Python/filesystem-mcp/UPDATE_STATUS.txt
.fastembed_cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# --- Local / machine-specific ---
# Per-build telemetry appended by build_indexes.py. Machine-specific operational
# data with no meaning on another checkout — tracking it would conflict on every
# pull, and it is regenerated one line at a time by simply building the index.
/Python/build_history.jsonl
.env
.obsidian/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*.lnk

# --- Private-side root documents ---
# These sit at the root because their tools require it, or because they predate
# the split. Root is now the engine repository, so "not for publication" only
# holds if they are named here. Setting-name hit counts from the 2026-09-04
# sweep are in the comments.
#
# NB: .gitignore has no inline comments — a trailing "# ..." becomes part of the
# pattern, silently matching nothing. Comments go above the line, never beside it.
#
# [4]  Codex requires it at the workspace root; its Purpose section names both
#      settings. A sanitized template is a possible later addition.
/AGENTS.md
# [49] Stale, and largely duplicates claude.ai userMemories.
/Claude_Code_Context.md
# [12] Belongs with Writing_Projects/, which is also private.
/Creative_Writing_Skills_Override.md
# [4]  Despite the name this is a fully populated save, not a skeleton: a
#      complete setting dump plus an in-progress story. Not a usable template.
/Core_Rules/Templates/template.ai-story.json

# --- Working documents: handoffs, proposals, plans. Contents never published. ---
# Ships the directory and its scaffolding, never its contents — same shape as
# World_Building. A directory rule rather than per-file entries, deliberately: a new
# handoff written here is excluded by default. The per-file version failed open —
# anything nobody remembered to list shipped.
#
# Use bare `*`, not `*.*`: `*.*` matches only names containing a dot, so a
# subdirectory such as Working_Documents/archive/ would not match, git would
# descend into it, and its contents would ship. Match the case exactly too — a
# lowercase negation appears to work where core.ignorecase is true, then inverts
# on a case-sensitive filesystem, hiding the README and publishing everything else.
#
# Three files are negated rather than one. README.md documents the convention; the
# other two make it work on a fresh clone, so that adopting it is `git init` and
# nothing else. Note that gitignore `*` matches leading dots — unlike shell globbing
# — so the dotfiles need explicit negations or they vanish with everything else.
#
# These three are the parent's, and the local repository inside this directory
# ignores all three in turn. That split is the whole convention: the engine repo owns
# the scaffolding that defines it, the local repo owns the documents it holds. Adding
# a file here means deciding which of those two it is.
/Working_Documents/*
!/Working_Documents/README.md
!/Working_Documents/.gitignore
!/Working_Documents/.gitattributes

# Homebrew rules sidecars are per-campaign and private by default.
homebrew_*
